  Rows marked NO did not converge. Their numbers are not
  evidence of anything -- do not read them as a method
  comparison. Raise --max-cycle or change the active space.

  largest deviation from the 6-root answer: 0.0335 eV
  -> state-specific reproduces the multi-state result. The restructuring
  is justified: build both surfaces state-specifically and spend the
  expensive multi-state machinery only on mu in the FC window.

========================================================================
== raster cost, per method (both surfaces, one geometry)
========================================================================
              method    wall/s     CPU/s    par       CPU-h  h/16 cores
            dCCSD(T)      18.6     594.1  31.9x       542.8        33.9
      dCASSCF+NEVPT2     449.0   14244.9  31.7x     13014.3       813.4

  Compare 03, the full multi-state point: 5783 CPU-s, 5283 CPU-hours,
  330 h on 16 cores. The speedup here is the whole argument.

  Remember this does NOT include mu_SOC, which still needs the multi-state
  treatment over the Franck-Condon window. At 5783 CPU-s per point, a few
  hundred FC points is 30 h on 16 cores -- so budget for that
  separately, and consider whether mu can be sampled coarsely and
  interpolated. water/10_mu_sensitivity.py measured what freezing mu costs:
  the peak moved 4 nm and the band narrowed 15%, so mu is not negligible but
  it is also not needed on the full raster grid.

